1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,730
Hi, and welcome!

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:06,290
Today we're going to learn how to hold a
pair of drumsticks the right way.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:09,130
Don't worry if you've never
touched a drum before.

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:12,610
We'll go one step at a time, and by
the end of this video,

5
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,570
you'll be ready to play your
very first beat.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,650
But first, a secret.

7
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,850
Most beginners hold their
sticks way too <b><i>tight.</i></b>

8
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:25,930
That one habit can hurt your sound, your
speed, and even your wrists.

9
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,210
So let's fix it from day one.

10
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,140
And stick around until the
end, pun intended,

11
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:33,490
because I've saved a <b><i>bonus</i></b> for you there.

12
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:36,100
All you need today is a pair of sticks.

13
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:41,250
If you're not sure which to buy, a size <b><i>5A</i></b>
is the most common starting point.

14
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,210
It's not too thick, and not too thin.

15
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:46,290
You don't need a full drum kit yet.

16
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:48,400
A practice pad works great.

17
00:00:48,430 --> 00:00:51,810
A firm pillow works too, if you
want to start right now.

18
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,560
Sit up straight, with your feet
flat on the floor,

19
00:00:54,590 --> 00:00:58,640
and place your pad or pillow in front
of you at about waist height.

20
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:00,930
A drumstick has four parts.

21
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:03,650
The tip is the end that hits the drum.

22
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:07,120
Next is the shoulder, where the
stick starts to narrow.

23
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:09,520
The long middle part is the shaft.

24
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:12,050
And the end you hold is called the butt.

25
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,810
Remember that last one, because the
butt end is where we'll start.

26
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:18,050
Every stick has a <b><i>balance</i></b> point.

27
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:20,610
In drumming, we call it the <b><i>fulcrum.</i></b>

28
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:24,450
It's the spot where the stick feels
light and bouncy in your hand.

29
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,650
To find it, rest the stick across one finger
and slide it until it balances.

30
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,570
For most sticks, that's about a third of
the way up from the butt end.

31
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,780
Now pinch the stick at that spot,

32
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:38,690
between your thumb and the side
of your first finger.

33
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,410
Think of pinching a pencil, only lighter.

34
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:43,410
Just enough that it won't fall.

35
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,410
This pinch point is your <b><i>pivot.</i></b>

36
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,290
The stick swings around it, like a seesaw.

37
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:49,250
Quick check.

38
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:52,050
Can you wiggle the stick back
and forth freely?

39
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,730
If so, great.

40
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,770
If it feels stuck, you're
squeezing too hard.

41
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,130
Loosen up a little.

42
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,420
Now bring in your other fingers.

43
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,080
Curl your middle, ring, and pinky
fingers around the stick,

44
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:06,050
and let them rest there loosely.

45
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:09,090
A good way to picture it is
holding a small <b><i>bird.</i></b>

46
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:13,410
Firm enough that it won't fly away, and
soft enough that you won't hurt it.

47
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:17,090
Your fingers should touch the stick,
but not clamp down on it.

48
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:21,090
And the end of the stick should poke out
just a little past your palm.

49
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:25,170
Next, turn your hands so your palms
face down toward the drum.

50
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,250
Your thumbs should sit along the side of
the stick, not on top of it.

51
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,960
Bring the tips of the sticks close
together near the middle of the pad,

52
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:34,930
so they make a wide V shape.

53
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:40,130
This is called <b><i>matched</i></b> grip, because both
hands hold the stick the same way.

54
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,730
It's the most common grip, and a
great place to start.

55
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:46,930
Here's a fun test to see if
your grip is <b><i>relaxed.</i></b>

56
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:50,770
Hold the stick a few inches above
the pad, and let it drop.

57
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,130
Don't push it down.

58
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:55,570
Just let go of the lift and let
gravity do the work.

59
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,610
If your grip is loose, the stick will
bounce back up on its own.

60
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:04,050
That bounce is called <b><i>rebound,</i></b> and it's
what makes drumming feel easy.

61
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:08,250
If the stick just thuds and stops,
you're holding it too <b><i>tight.</i></b>

62
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:10,850
Relax your fingers and try again.

63
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:15,760
Once you can feel the bounce, try a few
slow taps with one hand at a time.

64
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:18,240
Let the stick do most of the work.

65
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:21,820
Let's look at five mistakes almost
every beginner makes.

66
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:24,160
Number one. The <b><i>death</i></b> grip.

67
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:26,890
Squeezing the stick as hard as you can.

68
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,920
The fix is to hold it like
that small <b><i>bird.</i></b>

69
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:34,050
Number two. Pressing your thumb
down on top of the stick.

70
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:37,970
Keep your thumb along the side,
and let it pinch lightly.

71
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,550
Number three. Holding the
stick at the very end.

72
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:44,390
This makes it wobbly and hard to control.

73
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:48,510
Go back to that <b><i>balance</i></b> point,
about a third of the way up.

74
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:52,590
Number four. Fingers sticking
out, away from the stick.

75
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:55,070
Keep them curled and resting on it.

76
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,700
And number five. Tense
shoulders and stiff wrists.

77
00:03:58,730 --> 00:04:03,920
Take a breath, drop your shoulders, and
shake out your hands before you play.

78
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:07,300
Stick with me for one more
minute, pun intended.

79
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:08,840
Your <b><i>bonus</i></b> is next.

80
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:14,010
You stuck around, pun intended, so here's
your <b><i>bonus:</i></b> two last secret tips.

81
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,250
Tip one is your daily practice.

82
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,280
Set a timer for one minute.

83
00:04:18,310 --> 00:04:23,800
Tap slowly, right, left, right, left,
and count one, two, one, two.

84
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:27,530
Keep everything loose, and listen
for that nice bounce.

85
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:31,010
Tip two: do this once a day,
not once a week.

86
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,690
A little every day beats one long session,

87
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,820
and your grip will start to
feel natural fast.

88
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:41,600
In the next video, we'll look at the drum
kit and what each piece does.

89
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:46,500
If this helped, subscribe, and I'll
see you in the next lesson.
